1
00:00:03,560 --> 00:00:05,190
I just read about an experiment

2
00:00:05,190 --> 00:00:07,120
designed to see if you could make two people fall

3
00:00:07,120 --> 00:00:08,850
in love in a matter of hours.

4
00:00:08,850 --> 00:00:10,400
That doesn't sound right.

5
00:00:10,400 --> 00:00:12,780
My research has shown that it takes three to five years

6
00:00:12,780 --> 00:00:15,020
of shameless begging.

7
00:00:15,680 --> 00:00:18,370
Honey, neither of us comes off good in that story.

8
00:00:18,830 --> 00:00:19,720
Yeah, I-I...

9
00:00:19,720 --> 00:00:21,760
I saw that article you're talking about.

10
00:00:21,760 --> 00:00:24,770
Uh, the participants ask each other a series of questions

11
00:00:24,770 --> 00:00:26,700
designed to promote intimacy.

12
00:00:26,700 --> 00:00:28,020
And then they finish it off by staring

13
00:00:28,020 --> 00:00:29,720
into each other's eyes for four minutes.

14
00:00:29,720 --> 00:00:31,330
Oh, that's nonsense.

15
00:00:31,330 --> 00:00:33,300
I proclaimed my love for you.

16
00:00:33,300 --> 00:00:34,970
And the last time I looked into your eyes

17
00:00:34,970 --> 00:00:37,990
was when you thought you had conjunctivitis.

18
00:00:38,420 --> 00:00:40,120
Other than the fact that I had it,

19
00:00:40,120 --> 00:00:42,840
that was a magical night.

20
00:00:42,980 --> 00:00:45,350
Raj, would you ever try an experiment like that with Emily?

21
00:00:45,350 --> 00:00:47,890
What? I don't need science to win her heart.

22
00:00:47,890 --> 00:00:51,090
I have my family's wealth for that.

23
00:00:51,090 --> 00:00:53,890
I'm telling you, you can't create love in a few hours.

24
00:00:53,890 --> 00:00:55,170
- Right? - Oh, careful.

25
00:00:55,170 --> 00:00:58,660
You're poking at the whole foundation of The Bachelor.

26
00:00:58,740 --> 00:01:01,260
Yeah, but we don't have to debate this.

27
00:01:01,260 --> 00:01:02,290
We're scientists.

28
00:01:02,290 --> 00:01:04,340
We can conduct our own research.

29
00:01:04,340 --> 00:01:07,630
Yeah, I propose that we imprison two street people.

30
00:01:07,630 --> 00:01:08,800
- No. - And...

31
00:01:10,160 --> 00:01:11,960
You didn't even let me finish.

32
00:01:11,960 --> 00:01:13,110
Forget it!

33
00:01:13,480 --> 00:01:16,100
So, you can experiment on all the apes you want.

34
00:01:16,100 --> 00:01:19,240
But I want to manipulate the emotions of two captive human beings,

35
00:01:19,240 --> 00:01:21,460
suddenly I'm the monster.

36
00:01:22,480 --> 00:01:25,160
Why don't you just do the test?

37
00:01:25,160 --> 00:01:28,040
In the interest of science, I'd be willing to.

38
00:01:28,040 --> 00:01:29,910
What? You're okay with an experiment where you have

39
00:01:29,910 --> 00:01:33,730
to answer awkward questions about your innermost feelings?

40
00:01:33,730 --> 00:01:35,030
Yes.

41
00:01:35,030 --> 00:01:37,480
Please, can I do it with him? Please?!

42
00:01:38,510 --> 00:01:41,210
I've been listening to Sheldon's feelings on things for ten years.

43
00:01:41,210 --> 00:01:43,510
Tag, you're it.

44
00:01:43,510 --> 00:01:45,560
Yeah, but what if the experiment works?

45
00:01:45,560 --> 00:01:47,300
I'm not gonna fall in love with Sheldon.

46
00:01:47,300 --> 00:01:48,300
That's what I said.

47
00:01:48,300 --> 00:01:49,930
Before I knew it, he pontificated his way

48
00:01:49,930 --> 00:01:52,110
right into my heart.

49
00:01:52,110 --> 00:01:54,820
Uh, fun fact-- "pontificate"

50
00:01:54,820 --> 00:01:56,740
comes from the Latin word "pontifex,"

51
00:01:56,740 --> 00:01:59,870
which means "bridge builder" or "pope."

52
00:02:02,170 --> 00:02:04,180
In love yet?

53
00:02:30,790 --> 00:02:32,760
Just out of curiosity, Penny,

54
00:02:32,760 --> 00:02:36,070
if this experiment does make us fall in love,

55
00:02:36,070 --> 00:02:39,270
would you drive me to Lake Geneva, Wisconsin

56
00:02:39,270 --> 00:02:41,600
for Gary Con?

57
00:02:42,480 --> 00:02:44,370
It's the only convention celebrating

58
00:02:44,370 --> 00:02:46,690
the life and work of Gary Gygax,

59
00:02:46,690 --> 00:02:50,530
the co-creator of Dungeons and Dragons.

60
00:02:50,530 --> 00:02:52,050
You know what? I can honestly say

61
00:02:52,050 --> 00:02:54,420
if we fall in love, not only will I drive you there,

62
00:02:54,420 --> 00:02:58,020
I will buy you all the dragon T-shirts you want.

63
00:02:59,020 --> 00:03:01,560
Okay, babe, let's do this.

64
00:03:04,880 --> 00:03:06,380
You guys are really being calm

65
00:03:06,380 --> 00:03:08,610
about Penny and Sheldon doing this thing.

66
00:03:08,920 --> 00:03:09,740
If it were me,

67
00:03:09,740 --> 00:03:11,290
I-I'd be a little nervous.

68
00:03:11,290 --> 00:03:11,940
Why?

69
00:03:11,940 --> 00:03:13,950
Well, even if the study's nonsense,

70
00:03:13,950 --> 00:03:16,170
I-I don't believe in tempting fate.

71
00:03:16,170 --> 00:03:17,860
Same reason I wouldn't use a Ouija board,

72
00:03:17,860 --> 00:03:20,320
or pick a fight with an Asian guy.

73
00:03:20,770 --> 00:03:22,430
He probably doesn't know karate, but...

74
00:03:22,430 --> 00:03:23,960
why risk it?

75
00:03:25,160 --> 00:03:26,370
I think we're safe.

76
00:03:26,370 --> 00:03:28,780
Well, that's what the bullies at Bruce Lee's high school thought.

77
00:03:28,780 --> 00:03:29,920
And then, bam!

78
00:03:29,920 --> 00:03:31,710
Karate.

79
00:03:32,840 --> 00:03:35,660
Well, are we just gonna sit here while they do the experiment?

80
00:03:35,660 --> 00:03:37,350
The two of us could do it.

81
00:03:37,350 --> 00:03:38,840
Yeah, sure, that might be fun.

82
00:03:38,840 --> 00:03:39,970
What's the first question?

83
00:03:39,970 --> 00:03:41,630
Hang on.

84
00:03:41,930 --> 00:03:42,960
Okay.

85
00:03:43,400 --> 00:03:46,610
"Given the choice of anyone in the world,

86
00:03:46,610 --> 00:03:49,380
whom would you want as a dinner guest?"

87
00:03:50,930 --> 00:03:53,640
I can honestly say Penny.

88
00:03:54,940 --> 00:03:56,210
Then I choose a janitor.

89
00:03:56,210 --> 00:03:58,700
'Cause I'm about to throw up.

90
00:04:00,690 --> 00:04:01,310
I'm in love.

91
00:04:01,310 --> 00:04:03,070
Let's do something else.

92
00:04:04,800 --> 00:04:06,550
Ooh, uh, Emily gets off work soon.

93
00:04:06,550 --> 00:04:08,070
Why don't the four of us go out?

94
00:04:08,070 --> 00:04:08,950
- Okay. - Sounds good.

95
00:04:08,950 --> 00:04:10,680
Should we call Howard and Bernadette?

96
00:04:10,680 --> 00:04:12,040
I don't know what time their plane gets in,

97
00:04:12,040 --> 00:04:14,150
but let me, let me shoot them a text.

98
00:04:14,150 --> 00:04:16,350
Did he say anything about the funeral?

99
00:04:16,350 --> 00:04:19,500
Not much, but he seemed to be in a pretty good place.

100
00:04:19,500 --> 00:04:20,620
Are you kidding me?!

101
00:04:20,620 --> 00:04:23,930
You lost my mother's ashes?!

102
00:04:24,390 --> 00:04:27,620
No, I-I'm just saying that sometimes bags are misrouted.

103
00:04:27,620 --> 00:04:29,560
All right, fine. Where did you misroute

104
00:04:29,560 --> 00:04:31,450
the only woman who ever loved me?

105
00:04:31,450 --> 00:04:34,360
The first... well, first, I meant first.

106
00:04:35,700 --> 00:04:37,430
I just need some information.

107
00:04:37,430 --> 00:04:38,870
Uh, what's the flight number?

108
00:04:38,870 --> 00:04:40,470
816.

109
00:04:40,470 --> 00:04:41,920
I really did mean first.

110
00:04:41,920 --> 00:04:43,690
Just drop it.

111
00:04:44,570 --> 00:04:46,300
And can you describe the bag?

112
00:04:46,300 --> 00:04:47,710
Um, well, it's, uh, black.

113
00:04:47,710 --> 00:04:49,350
There's a red ribbon tied to the handle...

114
00:04:49,350 --> 00:04:52,490
The world's greatest mom is in the shoe compartment!

115
00:04:53,940 --> 00:04:55,150
You ready to begin?

116
00:04:55,150 --> 00:04:56,400
Yup. Be right there.

117
00:04:56,400 --> 00:04:58,190
I assume you don't want wine.

118
00:04:58,190 --> 00:04:59,090
Correct.

119
00:04:59,090 --> 00:05:00,590
You're not supposed to drink alcohol

120
00:05:00,590 --> 00:05:02,930
when operating heavy machinery.

121
00:05:02,930 --> 00:05:04,930
What heavy machinery?

122
00:05:08,690 --> 00:05:10,100
Let's just start.

123
00:05:10,100 --> 00:05:12,010
Well, as a Texas gentleman,

124
00:05:12,010 --> 00:05:14,050
I'm inclined to say ladies first.

125
00:05:14,050 --> 00:05:17,530
Although, I'm concerned that level of politeness and charm

126
00:05:17,530 --> 00:05:20,860
might make you fall in love with me before the test even begins.

127
00:05:20,860 --> 00:05:22,350
Perhaps we should flip a coin.

128
00:05:22,350 --> 00:05:24,340
Or if you're familiar with the rules

129
00:05:24,340 --> 00:05:27,650
- of Rock, Paper, Scissors... - Question one.

130
00:05:28,200 --> 00:05:29,870
"Given the choice of anyone in the world,

131
00:05:29,870 --> 00:05:32,230
whom would you want as a dinner guest?"

132
00:05:33,850 --> 00:05:35,370
Living or dead?

133
00:05:35,370 --> 00:05:36,650
Just says "Anyone in the world."

134
00:05:36,650 --> 00:05:37,800
I guess that means living.

135
00:05:37,800 --> 00:05:39,170
Ah, that's just as well.

136
00:05:39,170 --> 00:05:41,110
As much as I'd love to meet Euclid--

137
00:05:41,110 --> 00:05:42,630
inventor of the geometric proof--

138
00:05:42,630 --> 00:05:44,240
he probably wore sandals

139
00:05:44,240 --> 00:05:47,070
and I cannot look at toes during dinner.

140
00:05:48,340 --> 00:05:49,900
Oh, I know!

141
00:05:49,900 --> 00:05:52,080
The person I'd most like to have dinner with

142
00:05:52,080 --> 00:05:53,840
is myself.

143
00:05:55,730 --> 00:05:56,640
You sure that's your choice?

144
00:05:56,640 --> 00:05:58,840
'Cause I've had that dinner.

145
00:05:59,250 --> 00:06:00,390
Well, I haven't.

146
00:06:00,390 --> 00:06:02,180
And while they say never meet your heroes,

147
00:06:02,180 --> 00:06:04,670
I just don't see how I could disappoint.

148
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:06,390
Who would you choose?

149
00:06:06,390 --> 00:06:07,580
Robert Downey, Jr.

150
00:06:07,580 --> 00:06:08,500
You...

151
00:06:09,850 --> 00:06:11,900
I didn't think of Iron Man.

152
00:06:13,970 --> 00:06:15,950
You know, maybe after myself and I have dinner,

153
00:06:15,950 --> 00:06:18,350
we can meet you two for dessert.

154
00:06:21,510 --> 00:06:22,850
So, what do you guys want to do?

155
00:06:22,850 --> 00:06:25,080
Well, we're the ones tagging along, you pick.

156
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:28,080
Have you ever been to an escape room?

157
00:06:28,080 --> 00:06:28,970
Mm-mm, what's that?

158
00:06:28,970 --> 00:06:31,130
Um... it's kind of like interactive theater,

159
00:06:31,130 --> 00:06:32,460
except you have to solve puzzles

160
00:06:32,460 --> 00:06:34,190
in a certain amount of time to get out.

161
00:06:34,190 --> 00:06:35,480
There's one downtown where

162
00:06:35,480 --> 00:06:37,710
they trap you in a room with a zombie.

163
00:06:39,180 --> 00:06:42,500
So, kind of like what's happening with Penny right now.

164
00:06:45,600 --> 00:06:47,320
"What would constitute

165
00:06:47,320 --> 00:06:49,570
a perfect day for you?"

166
00:06:49,570 --> 00:06:51,190
Uh, well...

167
00:06:51,190 --> 00:06:54,700
I'd probably sleep in, do a little yoga,

168
00:06:54,700 --> 00:06:56,050
then lie on the beach while

169
00:06:56,050 --> 00:06:58,480
cute cabana boys brought me drinks and...

170
00:06:59,440 --> 00:07:00,670
probably get a massage

171
00:07:00,670 --> 00:07:03,170
and then cap off the night with some dancing.

172
00:07:03,550 --> 00:07:04,450
That's it?

173
00:07:04,450 --> 00:07:05,690
Yeah, why?

174
00:07:05,690 --> 00:07:08,200
You didn't mention Leonard.

175
00:07:09,470 --> 00:07:11,090
He's there.

176
00:07:12,960 --> 00:07:14,250
I don't think so.

177
00:07:14,580 --> 00:07:16,290
Leonard can't stand yoga,

178
00:07:16,290 --> 00:07:18,900
the beach, massages or dancing.

179
00:07:18,900 --> 00:07:20,190
Yeah, well, he brought a book, okay?

180
00:07:20,190 --> 00:07:21,470
What's yours?

181
00:07:23,590 --> 00:07:25,490
Uh, I wake up.

182
00:07:25,490 --> 00:07:29,140
Uh, I enjoy some French toast with butter and syrup.

183
00:07:29,140 --> 00:07:32,210
Uh, then a wormhole opens,

184
00:07:32,290 --> 00:07:36,300
and whisks me millions of years into the future

185
00:07:36,300 --> 00:07:39,030
where my towering intellect is used to save

186
00:07:39,030 --> 00:07:41,280
the last remnants of mankind

187
00:07:41,280 --> 00:07:44,240
from a predatory alien race.

188
00:07:45,450 --> 00:07:48,100
Interesting, you didn't mention Amy.

189
00:07:49,260 --> 00:07:52,140
Who do you think made the French toast with butter and syrup?

190
00:07:54,040 --> 00:07:55,340
You're about to enter the lab

191
00:07:55,340 --> 00:07:57,880
of the late Dr. David Saltzberg.

192
00:07:57,880 --> 00:08:00,910
While conducting studies on slowing the aging process,

193
00:08:00,910 --> 00:08:04,790
there was a catastrophic accident and he died.

194
00:08:05,840 --> 00:08:08,000
Or did he?

195
00:08:09,020 --> 00:08:09,890
Ladies?

196
00:08:09,890 --> 00:08:11,690
Are you being polite or scared?

197
00:08:13,130 --> 00:08:14,910
Yup.

198
00:08:17,470 --> 00:08:18,560
Ah, what a room!

199
00:08:24,080 --> 00:08:25,180
This is cool.

200
00:08:25,180 --> 00:08:26,420
So, how do we start?

201
00:08:26,420 --> 00:08:29,020
We have to look for the clues hidden around the room.

202
00:08:29,020 --> 00:08:30,750
Uh, wasn't there supposed to be a zombie?

203
00:08:35,330 --> 00:08:37,030
Okay, let's hope one of the clues

204
00:08:37,030 --> 00:08:39,600
is written on a pair of clean underwear.

205
00:08:41,730 --> 00:08:43,250
Mr. And Mrs. Wolowitz?

206
00:08:43,250 --> 00:08:46,610
As far as I can tell, your bag arrived in Los Angeles.

207
00:08:46,610 --> 00:08:47,790
So, where is it?

208
00:08:47,790 --> 00:08:49,500
I don't know, perhaps somebody

209
00:08:49,500 --> 00:08:51,890
took it off the carousel by mistake?

210
00:08:52,440 --> 00:08:55,290
So, some stranger has my mom?

211
00:08:55,290 --> 00:08:56,240
Is that what you're telling me?

212
00:08:56,240 --> 00:09:00,460
My poor mother can be anywhere in Los Angeles right now?

213
00:09:00,460 --> 00:09:03,680
I-I wish I was telling you that.

214
00:09:03,680 --> 00:09:05,690
Um...but the passenger could've gotten

215
00:09:05,690 --> 00:09:08,090
on an international flight.

216
00:09:08,760 --> 00:09:09,800
Oh, okay, great!

217
00:09:09,800 --> 00:09:12,280
So, your entire job is to find lost luggage,

218
00:09:12,280 --> 00:09:13,770
and you've narrowed down the location

219
00:09:13,770 --> 00:09:16,740
of my mother to the planet Earth!

220
00:09:17,200 --> 00:09:18,430
I'm sorry!

221
00:09:18,430 --> 00:09:20,940
W-Would 500 frequent-flyer miles help?

222
00:09:20,940 --> 00:09:23,550
That could get you to Sacramento.

223
00:09:26,100 --> 00:09:28,330
"If you could wake up tomorrow

224
00:09:28,330 --> 00:09:31,770
having gained any one quality or ability,

225
00:09:31,770 --> 00:09:33,460
what would it be?"

226
00:09:33,460 --> 00:09:35,390
Well, not to steal from the Bible,

227
00:09:35,390 --> 00:09:38,570
but turning water into wine sounds pretty good.

228
00:09:41,840 --> 00:09:44,590
I don't think you're taking this seriously.

229
00:09:45,140 --> 00:09:47,300
Come on, I'm just having some fun with you.

230
00:09:47,300 --> 00:09:49,180
I believe what you're doing

231
00:09:49,180 --> 00:09:53,050
is using humor to avoid vulnerability.

232
00:09:53,610 --> 00:09:54,920
Fine.

233
00:09:55,890 --> 00:09:57,400
Honestly...

234
00:09:57,860 --> 00:09:59,540
if I could have one quality...

235
00:10:00,130 --> 00:10:03,640
I wish I could be as smart as you guys.

236
00:10:03,640 --> 00:10:05,950
Ha! Keep dreaming.

237
00:10:07,430 --> 00:10:08,240
Sheldon!

238
00:10:08,240 --> 00:10:10,010
I'm sorry.

239
00:10:10,010 --> 00:10:12,210
That was me having fun with you.

240
00:10:12,210 --> 00:10:13,330
Look.

241
00:10:13,330 --> 00:10:15,450
You may not be as...

242
00:10:15,450 --> 00:10:18,510
academically inclined as are we.

243
00:10:18,510 --> 00:10:21,060
Yes, that's how you say it.

244
00:10:21,770 --> 00:10:22,450
But...

245
00:10:23,620 --> 00:10:26,480
You possess an intelligence I envy.

246
00:10:26,790 --> 00:10:29,000
Which leads me to my answer.

247
00:10:29,000 --> 00:10:30,980
I would choose the ability

248
00:10:30,980 --> 00:10:33,400
to read people's minds.

249
00:10:33,740 --> 00:10:36,680
Well, I can't read people's minds.

250
00:10:36,680 --> 00:10:37,860
Actually, that's not true.

251
00:10:37,860 --> 00:10:39,050
I can read men's minds.

252
00:10:39,050 --> 00:10:41,760
But only 'cause it's usually the one thing.

253
00:10:43,240 --> 00:10:45,900
When are we going to get robot eyes?

254
00:10:47,020 --> 00:10:49,780
You're all alike.

255
00:10:50,240 --> 00:10:52,280
Well, what I meant was...

256
00:10:52,720 --> 00:10:54,670
I often misinterpret

257
00:10:54,670 --> 00:10:56,300
how others are feeling.

258
00:10:56,300 --> 00:10:59,630
Like, I can't always tell if someone is only joking

259
00:10:59,630 --> 00:11:01,110
or laughing at me.

260
00:11:01,110 --> 00:11:03,800
You know, like, uh, if they're mad at something I've done

261
00:11:03,800 --> 00:11:05,420
or just in a bad mood.

262
00:11:05,420 --> 00:11:07,880
It-it's incredibly stressful.

263
00:11:07,880 --> 00:11:10,200
Really? You always seem so confident.

264
00:11:10,200 --> 00:11:11,380
Well, I'm not.

265
00:11:11,380 --> 00:11:14,320
And if I could read people's minds,

266
00:11:14,320 --> 00:11:16,320
life would be so much simpler.

267
00:11:17,730 --> 00:11:20,540
Well, now I wish I had the ability

268
00:11:20,540 --> 00:11:23,030
to make that stuff easier for you.

269
00:11:24,660 --> 00:11:25,650
Thank you.

270
00:11:29,710 --> 00:11:30,930
Wow, I just felt

271
00:11:30,930 --> 00:11:33,910
this wave of affection for you.

272
00:11:35,950 --> 00:11:38,660
You sure it's not too much Bible juice?

273
00:11:41,260 --> 00:11:43,890
And the wave is gone.

274
00:11:47,560 --> 00:11:48,840
Brains!

275
00:11:48,840 --> 00:11:51,550
Keep it down! We're working here.

276
00:11:51,550 --> 00:11:53,370
Okay, we've got the cipher decoded!

277
00:11:53,370 --> 00:11:54,610
How's it going with the globe?

278
00:11:54,610 --> 00:11:56,610
We used the coordinates to locate the cities.

279
00:11:56,610 --> 00:11:58,130
I'm putting the city names in the grid now.

280
00:11:58,130 --> 00:11:59,920
I'm sure that'll give us the code to the safe.

281
00:11:59,920 --> 00:12:01,760
Solve puzzle too fast!

282
00:12:01,760 --> 00:12:03,450
Slow down!

283
00:12:03,970 --> 00:12:05,360
Yup! Got the code.

284
00:12:05,360 --> 00:12:06,560
Just saying...

285
00:12:06,560 --> 00:12:10,130
no refund for finish early!

286
00:12:14,000 --> 00:12:15,870
Sure you don't want to go home?

287
00:12:15,870 --> 00:12:18,160
When the bag's returned, they'll deliver it to us.

288
00:12:18,160 --> 00:12:21,300
No, I'm not leaving without her.

289
00:12:21,300 --> 00:12:23,990
All right, we'll wait.

290
00:12:27,160 --> 00:12:29,130
I could've driven her.

291
00:12:29,130 --> 00:12:30,660
What?

292
00:12:31,050 --> 00:12:33,600
The day she left for Florida.

293
00:12:34,350 --> 00:12:37,130
She asked me to drive her to the airport.

294
00:12:39,300 --> 00:12:41,730
I-I was too busy.

295
00:12:41,730 --> 00:12:44,300
And...I made her

296
00:12:44,300 --> 00:12:46,710
take a cab.

297
00:12:48,260 --> 00:12:50,800
I was too busy.

298
00:12:51,320 --> 00:12:54,030
There's no way you could've known.

299
00:12:56,120 --> 00:12:58,050
Be right back.

300
00:13:00,580 --> 00:13:01,860
Excuse me?

301
00:13:02,120 --> 00:13:03,200
Yes?

302
00:13:05,320 --> 00:13:06,900
You better find my husband's mother

303
00:13:06,900 --> 00:13:09,180
'cause one way or another, we're walking out of this airport

304
00:13:09,180 --> 00:13:11,350
with a dead woman!

305
00:13:16,880 --> 00:13:18,290
Which book are we looking for?

306
00:13:18,290 --> 00:13:19,990
Origin of Species.

307
00:13:19,990 --> 00:13:20,890
Here it is.

308
00:13:22,360 --> 00:13:23,330
There's a black light.

309
00:13:23,330 --> 00:13:24,250
Oh, hang on.

310
00:13:27,920 --> 00:13:28,710
Oh, okay.

311
00:13:28,710 --> 00:13:31,170
Uh, "Brothers and sisters I have none,

312
00:13:31,170 --> 00:13:33,970
but this man's father is my father's son.

313
00:13:33,970 --> 00:13:35,930
Who am I looking at?"

314
00:13:37,490 --> 00:13:39,040
Yeah, yeah, we get it-- you want brains.

315
00:13:39,040 --> 00:13:40,660
Calm down.

316
00:13:40,660 --> 00:13:42,320
Well, if I don't have a brother,

317
00:13:42,320 --> 00:13:43,980
my father's son is me.

318
00:13:43,980 --> 00:13:47,350
And if I'm this man's father, then he's my son.

319
00:13:47,350 --> 00:13:48,420
The answer's "son."

320
00:13:48,420 --> 00:13:49,850
Ooh! There's a picture of the sun over there.

321
00:13:49,850 --> 00:13:51,110
I bet the key's behind it.

322
00:13:51,110 --> 00:13:52,550
Could be somewhere else!

323
00:13:54,030 --> 00:13:55,240
Oh, got the key!

324
00:13:55,240 --> 00:13:57,200
So, that's the key to the door?

325
00:13:57,200 --> 00:13:58,480
That's it?

326
00:13:58,480 --> 00:14:01,510
We spent $200 on six minutes of fun?

327
00:14:01,510 --> 00:14:03,880
It's like when you bought that remote-controlled helicopter,

328
00:14:03,880 --> 00:14:07,940
and it just... flew away.

329
00:14:07,940 --> 00:14:09,220
Sorry, guys.

330
00:14:09,220 --> 00:14:11,390
Really thought the puzzles would be better.

331
00:14:11,390 --> 00:14:15,070
Well, to be fair, we do all have advanced degrees.

332
00:14:15,480 --> 00:14:18,630
Remember that before you post on Yelp!

333
00:14:21,030 --> 00:14:23,580
"If you were to die this evening with no opportunity

334
00:14:23,580 --> 00:14:24,960
to communicate with anyone,

335
00:14:24,960 --> 00:14:28,500
what would you most regret not having told someone?"

336
00:14:28,930 --> 00:14:31,040
So, it would be today?

337
00:14:33,540 --> 00:14:35,470
Well, I suppose there's something satisfying

338
00:14:35,470 --> 00:14:38,020
about dying on my birthday.

339
00:14:38,020 --> 00:14:39,940
Today's your birthday?

340
00:14:39,940 --> 00:14:40,900
Yes.

341
00:14:40,900 --> 00:14:43,180
Well, that's always been a secret!

342
00:14:43,180 --> 00:14:45,020
Not even Amy knows!

343
00:14:45,020 --> 00:14:48,700
Well, I don't enjoy presents.

344
00:14:48,700 --> 00:14:50,150
And the thought of people

345
00:14:50,150 --> 00:14:52,600
jumping out and yelling "Surprise!"

346
00:14:52,600 --> 00:14:54,680
fills me with more dread than the words

347
00:14:54,680 --> 00:14:57,980
"George Lucas Director's Cut."

348
00:14:58,470 --> 00:15:00,940
So, why did you finally tell me?

349
00:15:01,430 --> 00:15:02,960
The point of the experiment is

350
00:15:02,960 --> 00:15:05,380
to be completely honest with each other.

351
00:15:06,240 --> 00:15:08,780
Well, thank you for sharing it with me.

352
00:15:08,780 --> 00:15:10,500
I won't tell anyone.

353
00:15:10,500 --> 00:15:12,030
Thank you.

354
00:15:12,030 --> 00:15:13,500
That is so funny.

355
00:15:13,500 --> 00:15:15,350
I never would have pegged you for a Pisces.

356
00:15:15,350 --> 00:15:19,000
You're making it difficult to love you right now.

357
00:15:21,320 --> 00:15:23,970
Great news! Your bag was returned.

358
00:15:23,970 --> 00:15:25,220
Oh, thank God!

359
00:15:25,220 --> 00:15:26,840
It's okay, she's here!

360
00:15:26,840 --> 00:15:28,040
Ma's here.

361
00:15:28,040 --> 00:15:29,820
Okay, thank you so much.

362
00:15:29,820 --> 00:15:30,970
Ma?

363
00:15:30,970 --> 00:15:34,740
I'm sorry I didn't take you to the airport.

364
00:15:34,740 --> 00:15:35,500
I just want you to know

365
00:15:35,500 --> 00:15:38,680
that I'll never forgive myself for being so selfish.

366
00:15:38,680 --> 00:15:40,230
And I promise

367
00:15:40,230 --> 00:15:41,900
to keep you close

368
00:15:41,900 --> 00:15:44,150
for the rest of my life.

369
00:15:45,180 --> 00:15:49,580
Oh, no. That thing's gonna end up in my bedroom.

370
00:15:51,370 --> 00:15:53,290
That's it! We're done with the questions.

371
00:15:53,290 --> 00:15:55,560
All that's left to do is stare into each other's eyes

372
00:15:55,560 --> 00:15:57,090
for four minutes without talking.

373
00:15:57,090 --> 00:15:57,870
Okay.

374
00:15:57,870 --> 00:15:59,040
Wait, hang on!

375
00:15:59,040 --> 00:16:00,680
Bladder check.

376
00:16:02,690 --> 00:16:04,460
We're good to go.

377
00:16:06,270 --> 00:16:07,030
You ready?

378
00:16:07,030 --> 00:16:07,710
Yes.

379
00:16:08,410 --> 00:16:11,240
And begin.

380
00:16:25,590 --> 00:16:28,520
This is kind of creepy.

381
00:16:29,130 --> 00:16:30,490
We're not supposed to talk during this part.

382
00:16:30,490 --> 00:16:31,730
Sorry.

383
00:16:39,000 --> 00:16:40,990
This is kind of creepy.

384
00:16:42,200 --> 00:16:43,360
Do you want to stop?

385
00:16:43,360 --> 00:16:45,380
I know you have trouble with eye contact.

386
00:16:45,380 --> 00:16:50,470
Well, you have a brown fleck in your right iris

387
00:16:50,470 --> 00:16:54,150
that looks like a Formula One race car.

388
00:16:54,150 --> 00:16:58,320
So... I'm just concentrating on that.

389
00:16:59,010 --> 00:17:00,440
Plus, it's easier around people

390
00:17:00,440 --> 00:17:02,280
that I'm comfortable with.

391
00:17:02,280 --> 00:17:05,680
Aw, sweetie, I'm comfortable around you, too.

392
00:17:05,680 --> 00:17:08,970
Of course you are, I'm warm and soothing.

393
00:17:10,040 --> 00:17:13,470
I'm like a human bowl of tomato soup.

394
00:17:14,350 --> 00:17:16,050
I meant more like a little brother.

395
00:17:18,610 --> 00:17:21,940
Well, I suppose I do think of you as a sister.

396
00:17:22,860 --> 00:17:25,350
And sometimes, a mother.

397
00:17:26,200 --> 00:17:27,900
It's getting creepy again.

398
00:17:35,950 --> 00:17:37,200
What?

399
00:17:37,200 --> 00:17:40,400
Just thinking about the day I met you and Leonard.

400
00:17:40,400 --> 00:17:42,530
It was a Monday afternoon.

401
00:17:42,530 --> 00:17:45,010
You joined us for Indian food.

402
00:17:45,490 --> 00:17:47,490
Can you believe it's been eight years?

403
00:17:47,490 --> 00:17:50,580
Yeah, and you're still eating our food.

404
00:17:54,240 --> 00:17:56,180
I... can't remember a time

405
00:17:56,180 --> 00:17:58,980
you guys weren't in my life.

406
00:17:58,980 --> 00:18:01,630
I remember it perfectly.

407
00:18:02,120 --> 00:18:05,060
But I have an eidetic memory.

408
00:18:05,060 --> 00:18:07,370
If you're interested, I also remember

409
00:18:07,370 --> 00:18:09,730
how much you owe us for the food.

410
00:18:14,760 --> 00:18:15,910
That's it.

411
00:18:16,250 --> 00:18:17,380
That wasn't so bad.

412
00:18:17,380 --> 00:18:18,930
No, it wasn't.

413
00:18:18,930 --> 00:18:22,640
Uh, now let's tabulate the results of the experiment.

414
00:18:23,410 --> 00:18:27,150
I think it's safe to say that you're not in love with me

415
00:18:27,150 --> 00:18:29,290
and I'm not in love with you.

416
00:18:29,290 --> 00:18:31,700
And psychology has once again

417
00:18:31,700 --> 00:18:35,670
proved itself the doofus of the sciences.

418
00:18:37,020 --> 00:18:38,090
Well, maybe.

419
00:18:38,090 --> 00:18:40,420
But I'm still glad we did it.

420
00:18:40,420 --> 00:18:43,190
I do feel closer to you.

421
00:18:43,880 --> 00:18:45,530
And I, you.

422
00:18:45,880 --> 00:18:49,720
And yes, that's how you say that.

423
00:18:50,520 --> 00:18:54,180
Yeah, so, given our newfound intimacy,

424
00:18:54,180 --> 00:18:56,740
I'd say we have some hard choices to make.

425
00:18:56,740 --> 00:18:57,880
Like what?

426
00:18:57,880 --> 00:19:01,100
Gary Con-- do we fly or drive?

427
00:19:01,100 --> 00:19:03,380
Do we wear costumes?

428
00:19:03,380 --> 00:19:05,170
And if so...

429
00:19:05,530 --> 00:19:07,860
who gets to be Gary?

430
00:19:12,790 --> 00:19:14,960
Thank you for walking me home.

431
00:19:14,960 --> 00:19:17,360
I just want to make sure you get there safe.

432
00:19:19,030 --> 00:19:22,380
Well, this is me.

433
00:19:23,500 --> 00:19:25,570
It's been a very interesting evening.

434
00:19:25,570 --> 00:19:27,520
It really has.

435
00:19:30,160 --> 00:19:32,350
Surprise!

436
00:19:35,140 --> 00:19:38,160
And after I let you be Gary!

